sents today for a high blood pressure check.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She has been on Norvasc 5 mg daily, which was increased to 10 mg daily. Her blood pressures have been elevated at home. She has been experiencing headaches, which she is not sure if it is due to stress at work or her hypertension. She has a history of a kidney transplant a few years ago, which is doing well. She is compliant with her autoimmunesuppression medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\nNeurological: Endorses headaches.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: No focal deficits.\nCardiovascular: Irregular rate and rhythm. Slight 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nMusculoskeletal: No lower extremity edema.\nSkin: No rashes or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nKidney function is normal post transplant.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nSusan Johnson is a 26-year-old female who presents today for a high blood pressure check.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood pressures are elevated. She has been on Norvasc 5 mg daily, which was increased to 10 mg daily, but her blood pressures remain elevated.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a lipid panel and a complete metabolic panel.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start her on carvedilol 25 mg twice a day.\n\nRenal transplant.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with normal kidney function.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood pressure.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSusan Johnson is a 26-year-old female who presents for a high blood pressure check. She has been struggling with high blood pressure her whole life, but it is seeming to get really out 