ahead and do a quick physical exam on you . so , hey dragon , show me the blood pressure . your blood pressure\'s actually pretty high today , so you might be a little nervous here , which is not uncommon .\n[patient] yeah .\n[doctor] um , so , looking at you , your , your neck is nice and soft , there\'s no enlarged thyroid . your heart has a regular rate and rhythm . your lungs are clear . your abdomen is nice and soft , but looking at your knees , on your left knee you do have a slight erythema and edema . there is a small efusion present over your left knee . um , you have some decrease flexion and extension of your knee , but your strength in your lower extremities is good . uh , so let\'s go ahead and look at some of your results . i know that we did some x-rays when you came in .\n[patient] yeah .\n[doctor] hey dragon , show me the left knee x-ray . okay , so looking at this , this is an , this is a normal x-ray of your left knee , so you may just have a little bit of a strain going on in your , in your knees there .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] uh , let\'s look at some of your labs , \'cause i know that you have the diabetes . hey dragon , show me the labs . so , here your white blood cell count is not elevated , so i\'m not concerned about an infection or anything like that . um , hey dragon , show me the diabetes\' labs . okay and , and your a1c is a little elevated , but it\'s not , it\'s not terrible , so i think for right now we\'ll just continue you on the current regimen . um , so my plan from you , for you in terms of the joint pain , um , i\'d like to go ahead and , you know , we\'ll just send some autoimmune labs to work , work up your left knee pain and , uh , we\'ll work for you to do some physical therapy and i\'d like to go ahead and give you anti-inflammatory medication to help with the pain . does that sound okay ?\n[patient] that sounds great .\n[doctor] okay . hey dragon , order meloxicam 15 mg once a day . hey dragon , order a physical therapy referral . hey dragon , order an autoimmune profile . and then from a hypertension standpoint you , because your blood pressure is a little elevated today , i\'d like you to continue to monitor it at home and , you know , send me a report in the next couple of weeks sh- , you know , giving me the , the blood pressure reportings . do you have a blood pressure cuff at home ?\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] okay . and then , um , we\'ll go ahead and order a lipid profile for you . hey dragon , order a lipid profile . and then for , from a diabetes standpoint let\'s go ahead and , uh , continue you on your metformin 500 mg twice a day . okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] do you have any questions ?\n[patient] no , that\'s it .\n[doctor] okay . the nurse will be in soon . it was good to see you .\n[patient] nice seeing you too .\n[doctor] hey dragon , finalize the note .\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJanet Smith is a 33-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of joint pain.\n\nThe patient reports that she has been doing stair workouts, which she thought would help build muscle on her knees but has not helped. She states that she has been having bilateral knee pain. She denies any other joint pain, including her elbows and shoulders. The patient notes that she had a rotator cuff repair two years ago and has not had any problems since that time. She denies any numbness and tingling in her feet or swelling in her legs. She notes that she is being cautious of these symptoms due to her diabetes. The patient denies fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath. She notes that she has high blood pressure but it has not elevated since her joint pain. She is currently taking Norvasc 5 mg daily which she takes every day. She denies any new symptoms.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of rotator cuff repair 2 years ago.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she is currently doing stair workouts.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she takes Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness and tingling.\nEndocrine: Denies polyuria, polydipsia, or polyphagia. Reports elevated blood sugars. Type 2 diabetes.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Normal gait. No focal deficits.\nMusculoskeletal: Examination of the left knee: Slight erythema and edema. There is a small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the left knee. Strength is 5/5 in the lower extremities.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nSkin: No rashes or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee obtained today are normal.\n\nLabs: CBC shows a white blood cell count that is within normal limits. A1c is elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJanet Smith is a 33-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of joint pain.\n\nLeft knee pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient has been experiencing bilateral knee pain. Her x-rays were normal. She has a history of rotator cuff repair 2 years ago.\n• Additional Testing: We will order an autoimmune profile.\n• Medical Treatment: I will refer the patient to physical therapy. I will prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is elevated today.\n• Additional Testing: We will order a lipid profile.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n\nType 2 diabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her a1c is elevated.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJanet Jackson is a 33-year-old female who presents for evaluation of left knee pain. She states she has been doing stair workouts which she thought would help build muscle on her knees and help with her knee pain. She denies any other joint pain such as elbows or shoulders. She had a rotator cuff repair 2 years ago and denies any problems since that time. She denies any pins and needles in her feet, any swelling in her legs, or any fever or chills. She denies any nausea or vomiting, chest pains, or shortness of breath. She does have high blood pressure but has been monitoring it and it has not elevated since the onset of her joint pain. She takes Norvasc 5 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain. Denies other joint pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck\n- General Examination: No enlarged thyroid.\n• Respiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n• Cardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\n• Musculoskeletal\n- Examination of Lower Extremities: Examination of the left knee: Slight erythema and edema. Small effusion present over the left knee. Decreased flexion and extension of the knee. Good strength in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the left knee was obtained and is unremarkable.\n\nLabs\nWhite blood cell count is within normal limits. A1c is slightly elevated.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJanet Jackson is a 33-year-old female who presents for evaluation of left knee pain. She states she has been doing stair workouts which she thought would help build muscle on her knees and help with her knee pain. She denies any other joint pain such as elbows or shoulders. She had a rotator cuff repair 2 years ago and denies any problems since that time. She denies any pins and needles in her feet, any swelling in her legs, or any fever or chills. She denies any nausea or vomiting, chest pains, or shortness of breath. She does have high blood pressure but has been monitoring it and it has not elevated since the onset of her joint pain. She takes Norvasc 5 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nPhysical examination revealed slight erythema and edema of the left knee, a small effusion present over the left knee, decreased flexion and extension of the knee, and good strength in the lower extremities.\n\nX-ray of the left knee was obtained and is unremarkable.\nLabs were obtained today and are within normal limits except for a slightly elevated A1c.\n\nBased on the patient\'s history and physical exam, I have recommended the following:\n\nFor her left knee pain, we will send some autoimmune labs to work her up. I have also recommended physical therapy and anti-inflammatory medication.\n\nFor her hypertension, I would like her to continue monitoring her blood pressure at home and send me a report in a couple of weeks. We will also order a lipid profile and continue her on metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Janet Sullivan is a 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of joint pain.\n\nShe localizes the joint pain to her bilateral knees. The patient reports she has been doing stair workouts, which she thought was building muscle on her knees. She denies any pins and needles in her feet or swelling in her legs. The patient notes that she would look out for those symptoms because she has diabetes. She denies any fever or chills. She denies any nausea or vomiting. The patient denies any chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nShe notes she had a right shoulder rotator cuff repair about 2 years ago, but she has not had any problems since that point.\n\nShe reports that her diabetes are well-controlled.\n\nThe patient does have high blood pressure but she monitors it and takes Norvasc 5 mg a day. She notes her blood pressure has not been elevated since her joint pain started.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Negative for fever, chills\n• Cardiovascular: Negative for chest pain or lower leg swelling.\n• Respiratory: Negative for shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Negative for nausea, vomiting.\n• Musculoskeletal: Positive for Arthralgias\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nNeck\n• General Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly.\n\nRespiratory\n• Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n• Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n• Left knee: Slight erythema and edema. Small effusion present. Decreased flexion and extension. Strength in lower extremities is good.\n\nRESULTS\n\nViews: Four views left knee.\nIndication: Knee pain.\nInterpretation: There are no fractures, dislocations, or other abnormalities.\n\nPLAN\n\nMs. Janet Sullivan is a 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of joint pain.\n\nLeft knee strain.\n• Medical Treatment: We will order an autoimmune panel for further workup. I am going to refer her to physical therapy and prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once daily to help with pain.\n• Specialist Referrals: Physical therapy.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is elevated in office today, but has been well-controlled on Norvasc 5 mg daily based on home monitoring.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen and home monitoring. She will send me a report in the next couple of weeks. We will also order a lipid profile.\n\nDiabetes type II.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her hemoglobin a1C is slightly elevated, but her blood glucose levels have been well-controlled.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


