10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdural collection. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n• Gait impairment.\n• Urinary incontinence and urgency.\n• Cognitive impairment.\n\nPLAN\n\nWe will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAdult hydrocephalus.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He is here today for a shunt check-up. The patient was first seen in clinic on 5/12/2020. At that time, he presented with 8 to 10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. A spino catheter protocol was established in June 2020, and he underwent shunt surgery on 8/1/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level 2.0 was placed.\n\nThe patient reports that he has not been sick or hospitalized since his surgery 3 months ago.\n\nHe states that his walking has improved since the surgery. He is now able to walk to the dining room, which he could not do prior to the surgery. However, he still has times where he needs assistance from his daughter.\n\nHe reports that his bladder control has improved some since the surgery but has leveled out, so he is wearing diapers. There are no issues with sores from wearing the diaper and no decreased or painful urination.\n\nHe believes he is better cognitively. He states that he does not feel lost when talking to someone anymore.\n\nHe denies headaches or pain where the shunt is. He also denies pain in his belly.\n\nThe patient is accompanied by his daughter, Theresa, who assists him with his daily life.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nStrata shunt set to 2.0.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: Impaired ab