e other wash , when i would use it twice a day , i was drying out . but not with this one .\n[doctor] i see . okay . well then do what feels good . if you do notice that you\'re starting to get dry , then reduce to once per day .\n[patient] that sounds good .\n[doctor] so to review , um , continue using the same face wash , continue with metro cream , and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] and then just do it until it\'s gone , and then do it for once a day for another week before stopping .\n[patient] okay , sounds good .\n[doctor] all right . and i\'ll rewrite your instructions here .\n[patient] thank you so much .\n[doctor] if there are any other questions or you\'re getting different instructions , feel free to reach out to me and we\'ll clarify , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] but in general , doxycycline is such a low risk . we\'re not gon na have you on this forever . but especially since we\'re adding the tacrolimus , i expect it will clear quickly .\n[patient] sounds good .\n[doctor] all right . and i just wan na really help knock it out as fast as possible , since you\'ve been dealing with it for so long now . i would take it twice a day until it\'s gone , and then once a day for a week before you stop . i\'ll write it out like that . so twice a day until rash is gone , and then once a day for another week before stopping .\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] once the rash stops , you can stop the tacrolimus ointment . um , continue doxycycline another week and then stop . but you can keep using the wash and the metro cream .\n[patient] okay , that sounds good . i can do that .\n[doctor] and then hold off until i see you again before you start back any of your old products . um , if it\'s starting to flair again , um , if it\'s just a little bit , you can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple days , to see if that\'s enough . if it\'s not , then restart the dy- doxycycline as well .\n[patient] all right .\n[doctor] i do n\'t expect that to happen , but , you know , if it does , you have the tools . um , then you\'ll be seeing me for a followup . we can figure out what to do next . i would say if it\'s getting worse rather than better , just give me a call or send me a mychart message , okay ?\n[patient] yeah , sounds good .\n[doctor] do you have enough refills ?\n[patient] yeah , i think so .\n[doctor] okay . so it looks like you have another refill of the doxycycline , so you\'re good there . metro cream , you probably still have enough of that one .\n[patient] yes , i do .\n[doctor] all right . and i\'ll cancel the elidel cream , just so we do n\'t complicate your list . uh , you have plenty of the face wash still .\n[patient] yes .\n[doctor] okay . uh , and we\'ll get rid of the clindamycin from your list as well .\n[patient] and what would you suggest for a moisturizer ?\n[doctor] yeah , so for that i would do something really basic like vanicream . or even , if it\'s just really dry , you could use a little bit of petroleum jelly . um , i\'ll give you a couple samples , okay ? let\'s make sure we have a followup in about four weeks .\n[patient] okay , thank you .\n[doctor] okay . and i would stay away from any kind of anti-aging thing , any kind of plant thing . um , really let\'s just be sure we talk about any new products , or reach out to me if you\'re gon na try something else . um , let\'s go get those samples .\n[patient] okay , that sounds good .\n[doctor] for assessment and plan , perioral dermatitis , rosacea , including ocular rosacea , and copy and paste her patient instructions . i recommend that she gently wash her eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as vanicream , also use bruder mask as needed .\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 34-year-old female who presents today for a follow-up visit. The patient is here for perioral dermatitis. The patient was initially seen on 04/02/2021. The patient reports that she has been doing much better with her dermatitis. She has a small bit of a rash leftover. The patient started using Doxycycline 100 mg once a day because she thinks the pharmacist said if it is getting better, to just limit it to once a day. The patient was taking it twice a day and did notice a lot of improvement. She then started doing it only once a day and she says it seems the same and it is not continuing to get better. She is not breastfeeding at this time. She stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the Doxycycline at that time. She did not take the Elidel because she got worried when she read about it. She wanted to just try the Doxycycline and see.\n\nThe patient is using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She is not drying her out too much. She is using the metronidazole cream after her face wash. She is not using the Cetaphil cleanser. She is not using the Neutrogena wipes. She is not using any lotion right now, just those two. She went down to just taking the Doxycycline once a day last week. She did the first two weeks twice a day and then started seeing improvement and then changed to just once a day.\n\nThe patient is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On examination of the bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils. The patient is right, it is not totally gone.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s history, examination, and current status, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her dermatitis. I advised that I think to help get rid of it sooner rather than later, if insurance will cover it, she should pick up the Elidel. The patient did check and it is not covered. I told her to use a goodrx coupon, it will be around $30 to $40. I actually found the cream form. We could try the ointment form. She was concerned that it was not a steroid. I explained that it is not a steroid. It is a calcineurin inhibitor. It is kinda like a steroid in that it calms inflammation. It does not cause thinning of the skin or other kinds of rashes. The thing to know is that sometimes 5 to 10 minutes after you put it on, it can cause a weird kind of tingling or a needle-like sensation or make it redder or flush. But it should only last a few seconds and then go away. It is not an allergy or anything bad. It only lasts a couple of seconds. It does not mean it is going to happen again. It will still work, so keep using it as long as you know you can stand it, okay?\n\nI advised that she apply to the rash on her face twice daily until resolved. She may use the face wash once a day. I do not think she needs to do it twice a day because I do not want to dry her skin out too much. She does not think she is drying out, she feels good after using it. She said she never noticed that her face is dry or got thin. She feels so good after using it. I understand, but it could get dry and I do not want to create other problems for her.\n\nTo review, continue using the same face wash. Continue with the metronidazole cream. Increase the use of Doxycycline to twice a day. If she does notice that she is starting to get dry, then reduce to once a day. I will rewrite her instructions. If there are any other questions or she is getting different instructions, feel free to reach out to me and we will clarify. I want to really help knock it out as fast as possible, since she has been dealing with it for so long now. I would take it twice a day until it is gone, and then once a day for a week before stopping. I will write it out like that. So twice a day until rash is gone, and then once a day for another week before stopping. Once the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue Doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the metronidazole cream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nOnce the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue Doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the metronidazole cream. If it is starting to flair again, if it is just a little bit, she can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then restart the Doxycycline as well. She will be seeing me for a follow-up. We can figure out what to do next. I would say if it is getting worse rather than better, just give me a call or send me a MyChart message. She has enough refills.\n\nThe patient has another refill of the Doxycycline, so she is good there. Metro cream, she probably still has enough of that one. She does. I will cancel the Elidel cream just so we do not complicate her list. She has plenty of the face wash. I will get rid of the clindamycin from her list as well.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nOnce the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue Doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the metronidazole cream. If it is starting to flair again, if it is just a little bit, she can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then restart the Doxycycline as well. She will be seeing me for a follow-up. We can figure out what to do next. I would say if it is getting worse rather than better, just give me a call or send me a MyChart message. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nErythematous papules on bilateral medial cheeks.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a pleasant 41-year-old female who presents today for a follow-up visit. The patient reports that she is doing well. She is still having some residual rash leftover. She states that she started using doxycycline once a day because she believes that the pharmacist told her that if she was doing well, she should limit it to once a day. She states that she was taking it twice a day and noticed a lot of improvement, but when she started doing it once a day, it seems the same and it\'s not continuing to get better, and it\'s kind of plateaued a little bit. She denies breastfeeding.\n\nThe patient reports that she stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the doxycycline at that time. She states that she did not take the Elidel because when she read about it, she got worried. She is concerned about the side effects of Elidel.\n\nThe patient reports that she is not using the Cetaphil cleanser. She states that she is using the Sulfacet wash twice a day, and that is not drying her out too much. She states that she is also using the Metro cream after her face wash. She states that she is not using any lotions. She states that she stopped breastfeeding and started taking doxycycline.\n\nShe is currently using the Sulfacet wash twice a day. She states that she is using the Metro cream after her face wash. She is not using any lotions.\n\nThe patient reports that she is feeling sun sensitive whenever she goes out, so she is taking all precautions, wearing a hat and all of that.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nSkin\n• Examination of the skin shows Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. Bilateral medial cheeks have a few faint erythematous papules. There is a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis.\n• Rosacea.\n• Ocular rosacea.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s history and exam today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current condition. She is a Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V female who is currently experiencing erythematous papules on her bilateral medial cheeks. She has been using Sulfacet wash twice daily and Metro cream. We have decided to continue with this regimen. I have also recommended Tacrolimus ointment to be used twice a day until the rash is gone, and then once a day for another week before stopping. We will re-evaluate at her next appointment. She will discontinue Elidel cream. We discussed the use of topical steroids and the risk of skin thinning, as well as the use of oral steroids and the risk of hematologic cancers. We also discussed the use of calcineurin inhibitors.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe will gently wash her eyelids once a day with gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. She will also use Bruder mask as needed. She will continue to use Sulfacet wash once a day. She will continue to use Metro cream. She will use Tacrolimus ointment twice a day until the rash is gone, and then once a day for another week before stopping. She will hold off until I see her again before she starts back any of her old products. If it is starting to flare again, she can try just doing Tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough, if not, then restart the doxycycline as well. She will contact me if it is getting worse rather than better.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI have given the patient some samples of Vanicream.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nHISTORY OF MEDICATIONS\n\n• Doxycycline.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nShe has enough refills of the doxycycline, Metro cream, and Sulfacet wash.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nI have canceled the Elidel cream. I have also given the patient a few samples of Vanicream. I will see her in 4 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up for perioral dermatitis.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who is being seen today for a follow-up skin exam for perioral dermatitis. She also has a history of rosacea, including ocular rosacea. Today, she states that her perioral dermatitis is well-controlled, although it has not fully resolved. The patient was applying doxycycline twice a day with improvement, but her pharmacist advised her to reduce to once daily since it had started to improve; therefore, for approximately the past week, she has been using the doxycycline only once per day. She has not seen continued improvement but it has not worsened. She is using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily, denies drying of the skin, and reports that her skin is much improved moisturization. She also applies MetroCream immediately after using the sulfacetamide wash. She denies using other lotions and products, Cetaphil lotion or soap, or Neutrogena wipes. The patient did not begin Elidel because it is not covered by her insurance, and she was concerned about possible side effects from the medication.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nPerioral dermatitis.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nDoxycycline cream. Apply twice daily.\nMetroCream. \nSulfacetamide wash. Apply topically twice daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nIntegumentary\nSkin inspection of the face was performed today. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few, very faint, erythematous papules with mild redness around and underneath the nostrils. Fitzpatrick skin type is 4 to 5.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Perioral dermatitis\n• Rosacea, including ocular rosacea\n\nJacqueline Miller is a 40-year-old female who presented today for follow-up for her perioral dermatitis, which has improved but not resolved. She decreased her doxycycline per her pharmacist’s recommendation, which possibly stagnated the resolution of symptoms.\n\nPLAN\n\nPerioral dermatitis.\nI counseled the patient about the risks and benefits of doxycycline and recommended she resume twice daily application until she reaches full resolution, and at that point she should reduce application to once a day for an additional week. The patient will not be using Elidel as I reviewed the cost of the medication and without insurance, it was too expensive. I provided a prescription for Tacrolimus and counseled her on possible side effects of using the medication. She will continue with Sulfacetamide face wash once a day. Also, use Bruder mask as needed.\n\nRosacea, including ocular rosacea.\nContinue using MetroCream twice daily. I recommended to gently wash her eyelids once daily with a gentle cleanser and try a gentle cream such as Vanicream.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nCall the clinic if symptoms worsen. The patient will have a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


