f his abdomen, underneath his ribs, and is described as squeezing in nature. He rates his pain as an 8 out of 10 in severity. The pain is intermittent but is often associated with nausea and vomiting. The pain can occur immediately after eating as well as a couple of hours after eating. The patient states that he has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been following a low-carb diet for the past 3 months. He notes that his symptoms have improved with his diet but he is still experiencing right upper quadrant pain. He denies any weight loss, fever, or chills.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he enjoys photography and hiking. He states that he has been able to get outside and hike more since the weather is getting nice.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that his mother and father both had their gallbladder removed.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/88 mmHg\nRespiratory rate: 16\nPulse: 68\nTemperature: Afebrile\nOxygen saturation: 99% on room air\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, rubs, or murmurs.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.\nAbdomen: Positive for Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness. Slight guarding noted in the right upper quadrant. No peritoneal signs.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound demonstrates multiple gallstones in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder wall is noted. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nBobby Jackson is a 64-year-old male who presents today for evaluation of right upper quadrant pain. He has a history of type 2 diabetes and has been following a low-carb diet for the past 3 months. His symptoms have improved with his diet but he is still experiencing right upper quadrant pain. His physical exam is notable for positive Murphy\'s sign, slight guarding in the right upper quadrant without rebound tenderness. His ultrasound demonstrates multiple gallstones in the gallbladder and mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Given his symptoms and exam findings, I believe he has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. Given that he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. All questions were answered today.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with his primary care physician for his diabetes. He will follow up with me in 1 week for post-operative care. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of type 2 diabetes, which is currently managed with diet and exercise.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying photography and hiking.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a family history of gallbladder disease.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting.\nHENT: Denies headaches or shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports abdominal pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory rate: 16.\nPulse: 68.\nTemperature: Afebrile.\nOxygen saturation: 99% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, rubs, or murmurs.\nGastrointestinal: Positive bowel sounds. Soft, nondistended abdomen. Slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. Murphy\'s sign is present but no peritoneal signs.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound: Reveals multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild thickening of the gallbladder is appreciated. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has had persistent abdominal pain for several weeks. The ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder with some mild thickening. She has not had improvement with dietary modifications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that surgery may be necessary. We discussed the risks and benefits of laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n- Medical Treatment: We will proceed with laparoscopic cholecystectomy.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient is currently being managed with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to follow up with her primary care provider to ensure that she is managing her diabetes well, especially with the upcoming surgery.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue to manage her diabetes as she is currently.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\