d flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine showed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nJeremy Smith is a 47-year-old male who presents with lower back pain. After reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His examination was notable for bony protuberance at C5-6, decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise, and spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. His x-ray of the lumbar spine showed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nGiven his examination and radiographic findings, I have diagnosed him with other intervertebral disc degeneration with associated pain. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to further evaluate the extent of his disc degeneration.\n\nI have also recommended that he continue with physical therapy. I have referred him to a physical therapist through our health system.\n\nAdditionally, I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with his pain.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me after he completes his physical therapy and we will discuss further treatment options at that time. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of right knee replacement in 2020.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports moving boxes when his son was moving out of his college dorm, which resulted in the onset of his back pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nEyes: No visual changes.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain.\nGenitourinary: Denies urinary urgency or incontinence.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- Examination: Bony protuberance of C5-C6. No pain to palpation.\n\nLumbar Spine\n- Examination: No pain to palpation. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Sparing of paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Lower extremities: Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine was obtained and reviewed today. This revealed no acute bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Acute lumbar disk herniation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with a history of back pain that began 4 months ago. The pain is located in his lower back and radiates to his right foot. He has had no urinary or bowel incontinence. His exam is significant for decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, positive straight leg raise, and sparing of paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of his diagnosis, treatment options, and prognosis in detail. I advised the patient that he may require further treatment such as an epidural steroid injection if his current treatment does not improve his symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: I have prescribed Meloxicam 15 mg once a day to help with his pain. I also want him to follow up with physical therapy.\n\n2. Right knee replacement.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient underwent a right knee replacement in 2020.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of his diagnosis, treatment options, and prognosis in detail. I advised the patient that he may require further treatment such as an epidural steroid injection if his current treatment does not improve his symptoms.\n- Medical Treatment: None.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to re-evaluate his progress. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJeremy Adams is a pleasant 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 4 months ago, when he was moving boxes out of his son’s college dorm. At that time, he noted tingling in his right toes, which resolved after approximately 1 hour. After his injury, he felt as though he was unable to support his upper body weight. The patient reports weakness in his legs when carrying heavy objects. He reports a sharp pain in his lower back when he turns a certain way or rolls over in his sleep. The pain will resolve with repositioning. The patient was seen at urgent care and was referred to obtain an MRI, however he did not undergo this secondary to insurance issues. He has been utilizing Tylenol, ibuprofen, heat, and ice for pain, which does not provide relief. The patient notes the ibuprofen is slightly more beneficial than the Tylenol, however it does not relieve his symptoms. He denies upset stomach with the use of ibuprofen. Additionally, he attempted physical therapy at his gym without any relief. He denies any bladder or bowels issues, fevers, and chills.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of a right total knee arthroplasty 2 years ago, in spring of 2020. He states that his right knee feels 95% improved since the surgery.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\nGastrointestinal: Denies bowel issues.\nGenitourinary: Denies bladder issues.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower back pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nTemperature: Normal.\nBlood pressure: 117/56 mm Hg.\nHeart rate: 68 BPM.\nOxygenation: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Bony protuberance of the C5-C6. No pain to palpation of the C5-C6\nMSK: Examination of the Lumbar spine: No pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Decreased flexion and extension of the lower back. Positive straight leg raise. Spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine. Strength is equal bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine were reviewed. These reveal no acute bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nLow back pain, possible acute disc herniation.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. His x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture. I would like to obtain an MRI of the lumbar spine to evaluate for a possible acute disc herniation. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg to treat his pain. He will continue with physical therapy and a referral was provided for this today.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


