 right side i do n\'t hear any wheezing right now there is some diminished breath sounds in the right side as well on your heart exam you\'ve got two over successolic ejection murmur you\'ve had that in the past that\'s unchanged from before the rest regular rate and rhythm otherwise your belly exam is nice and soft on your extremity exam you\'ve got one plus nonpitting edema on both of your lower extremities on your on your ankles no calf tenderness no negative homan sign so what does all this mean so basically you\'ve got you know you\'ve got some sort of infection or something going on in your lungs that i can hear right now the rest of your exam is pretty much stable it\'s unchanged from before so let\'s talk about what we what we should do about this so for the for the first problem with the shortness of breath the first thing i wan na do is go ahead and get a chest x-ray for you okay you\'ve been coughing up blood i also wan na send you to the emergency department to get some blood testing done i\'m worried about a blood clot or something else going on so i wan na get a cat scan of your chest as well so i\'m gon na go ahead and refer you to the emergency department i\'ll call them and and have you head over that way they can get a chest x-ray and a cat scan and some blood work as well and then we\'ll evaluate why you\'re having the shortness of breath and why you\'re coughing up this blood okay and then depending on what they find you may need you know it could be as simple as a pneumonia or it could be something a little bit more serious we may have to get you know start you know depending on what you find we will get the right treatment started any questions about that or can you drive to the emergency department from here or is that okay\n[patient] yeah i can bike no problem i have no question\n[doctor] alright for the second problem for the diabetes since your blood sugar is running a little bit high once you get over this i\'m blood sugars running a little bit high once we get you over this hump of the shortness of breath and get this diagnosed i\'m gon na go ahead and order another hemoglobin a1c today we will probably increase your dose of metformin from five hundred twice a day to seven fifty twice a day so why do n\'t we do that and then why do n\'t we have we\'ll have the hemoglobin a1c back and then we can once you get over this illness that you\'re undergoing we\'ll we\'ll we\'ll discuss that more i\'ll have you come back in about three weeks to just discuss your diabetes and what our treatment plan will be for that okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] for the high blood pressure definitely hold off on the norvasc for right now let\'s figure out what\'s going what\'s causing this episode of shortness of breath i want some blood testing done and and once we have that under better control we can reassess your blood pressure so right now hold the norvasc and then we\'ll have you come back once everything is done okay\n[patient] no\n[doctor] any other questions or anything we know if we need to talk about today\n[patient] no i\'m good\n[doctor] sounds good we\'ll get you signed out here in a second\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Johnson is a 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of shortness of breath. He reports that he has been having shortness of breath for the last couple of weeks. The patient states that he wakes up short of breath, and when he gets home, he is short of breath again. He states that he has had a tough time dealing with this. The patient reports that he has been coughing up blood for the last three weeks. He denies any hemoptysis beyond that. He denies any hematemesis, hematuria, or melena. He reports that he has had a knee problem, but denies any leg swelling. He denies any recent travel history.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been to the hospital and the emergency room. He tried to get an appointment in the office, but they were booked up.\n\nThe patient is on metformin. He reports that his blood sugar has been going up and down. He wears a dexcom and reports that after meals, his blood sugar spikes up to 200. In the morning, it is around 150. He reports that his blood sugar has been high for the last month. The patient is on Norvasc. He states that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low. He reports that his blood pressure has been as low as the 90s.\n\nThe patient reports that he is short of breath when he walks. He denies any fevers.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath and hemoptysis.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies hematemesis, hematuria, or melena.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: 2/6 ejection murmur, unchanged from past.\n\nRESPIRATORY: Rales and rhonchi on auscultation of the right lung. Diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c: 7.7.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nShortness of breath.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with shortness of breath and hemoptysis. He has a history of asthma and hypertension. His exam is concerning for a lung issue. I have ordered a chest X-ray and a CT scan of the chest as well as blood work from the ED. The patient will follow up with me in the clinic once he is stable.\n• Medical Treatment: I have placed the patient on oxygen. He will be going to the emergency department today for further evaluation and treatment.\n\nDiabetes.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has been running high. His last hemoglobin A1c was 7.7.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to increase his metformin from 500 mg twice a day to 750 mg twice a day. I will order another hemoglobin A1c today.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is on Norvasc. He reports that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to hold the Norvasc until we get his blood pressure under better control. He will follow up with me once he is stable.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once he is stable. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, high blood pressure, and asthma.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a recent history of knee problem.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily and Norvasc.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fever.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath and coughing up blood.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain or blood in stool or urine.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Right lower lobe coarse rhonchi and rales.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No extra heart sounds, rubs, or gallops.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft abdomen.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema in both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No Homan\'s sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nHemoglobin A1c: 7.0.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Shortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient presents today with shortness of breath and coughing up blood. His oxygen saturation is normal, but he has some abnormal lung sounds.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that he has some type of infection or issue going on in his lungs. I also explained that his diabetes has been running a little high.\n- Medical Treatment: I have referred him to the emergency department for a chest x-ray, CT scan, and blood work. We will evaluate why he is having shortness of breath and coughing up blood.\n\n2. Elevated blood sugar.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood sugar has been running a little high lately.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that once he gets over his current illness, we will discuss a treatment plan for his diabetes.\n- Medical Treatment: I will order another hemoglobin A1c today. We will increase his metformin to 750 mg twice daily. We will have him come back in 3 weeks to discuss his diabetes.\n\n3. Elevated blood pressure.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood pressure has been running a little low.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained to the patient that we will reassess his blood pressure once we figure out what is causing his shortness of breath.\n- Medical Treatment: He will hold his Norvasc for now.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath with hemoptysis.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma. He also has a long history of knee pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin and Norvasc.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever.\nHENT: Denies bleeding gums.\nRespiratory: Reports shortness of breath, hemoptysis.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports knee pain. Denies lower extremity edema.\nHematologic/Lymphatic: Denies bruising.\n\nVITALS\n\nPulse oxygenation: 94 percent. Blood pressure: 120/70 mm Hg.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple. No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Some rales and rhonchi, more on the right. No wheezing right now. There is some diminished breath sounds in the right as well.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from before.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema on bilateral lower 