PLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Davis is a 45-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago. The patient was playing soccer over the weekend when he felt a pop in his right knee. He reports that his right knee is swollen. He rates his pain as 6 out of 10. The patient states that he can bend his knee, but it hurts at the end of the motion. He can straighten his knee, but it does not feel normal. He is able to bear weight on the knee, but it feels loose. He denies any numbness in his toes. He has been taking ibuprofen with some relief.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain. Denies any numbness in his toes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Gait: Marked limp on the right leg upon ambulation.\n- Examination of the right knee: I do appreciate some significant effusion. There is a slight bit of ecchymosis. On palpation, he is tender to the touch on the medial joint line. Range of motion is limited. He is lacking about 5 degrees of terminal extension to about 115 degrees. He is lacking about 20 to 30 degrees of terminal flexion. He has a positive Lachman exam.\n\nRESULTS\n\nN/A\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n- Right knee injury, likely anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nI have had the opportunity to evaluate the patient today. He sustained a right knee injury 3 days ago playing soccer. He is experiencing pain, swelling, and limited range of motion. His examination is consistent with an anterior cruciate ligament tear. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI to further evaluate the integrity of the ACL. We will review the results of the MRI and reassess his need for surgery. In the meantime, I am going to prescribe meloxicam 50 mg once a day. I would like him to ice the knee if he is still seeing swelling and pain. He should wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when walking. He should also limit his activities. I would like him to continue to work, but he should limit his work activities as well. He should not jog until further notice.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once we have the results of the MRI. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 30-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago after he tried to go out and play some pickup soccer games. He states that he went to go plank kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. He notes that it is swollen, some of the swelling has gone down. He also notes that his knee is not functioning as well as it used to, it seems to be a little limited. He rates his pain as 6-7 out of 10, currently 4-5 with some of the swelling going down. He states that he took anti-inflammatories which helped a little bit. He denies any weight bearing or walking on the leg. He notes that he can bend his knee, but it hurts towards the end of the motion. He can also straighten his leg, but it does not feel normal.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Examination of the right knee shows significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Palpation shows some pain to palpation at the medial joint line. Range of motion is limited, passive range of motion is limited to 115 degrees of knee extension and 40 degrees of knee flexion. There is a positive Lachman test.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee was taken and reviewed today in office. It was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee injury is suggestive of an ACL tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI to evaluate the integrity of his ACL. I have also recommended that he limit his activities, wear a knee immobilizer, and use crutches when walking. He will take meloxicam 50 mg once a day. He will ice the knee as needed. He will also continue to modify his work activities. He will not jog until we receive results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week to discuss the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week to discuss the results of his MRI. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
