ry of hypertension and is on 10 mg of lisinopril daily. She states that her blood pressures have been in the teens to thirties. She states that she takes her medication every day. The patient also has a history of diabetes and is on 500 mg of metformin twice a day. She states that she forgets to take it at times. The patient also has a history of baking and making cakes and cinnamon rolls.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nDiabetes\nHypertension\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that she enjoys baking and making pottery.\n\nCURRENT MEDICATIONS\n\nLisinopril 10 mg daily\nMetformin 500 mg twice a day\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\nLungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo murmurs, gallops, or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nExamination of the right elbow\nModerate tenderness to palpation of the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist. No rash or bruising. No swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow is reviewed and is unremarkable. No fracture or bony abnormality is noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n2. Diabetes.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to her current symptoms. I have explained to the patient that she is experiencing symptoms consistent with medial epicondylitis. I have recommended that she rest her arm and wear a sling while she is awake. I have also advised her to apply ice to her elbow for 20 minutes three times a day. I have prescribed ibuprofen 600 mg three times a day with food for one week. She is advised to refrain from pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n\n2. Diabetes.\nAfter reviewing her blood glucose levels and her current medication regimen, I have recommended that we increase her metformin to 1000