began about a week ago. He denies any injury. He tried over-the-counter medications with minimal relief. His pain is located in the lower back and radiates to his left side. The pain is constant and is aggravated with sudden movements. He denies any numbness or tingling in his lower extremities.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes. He is currently on metformin 500 mg twice daily. He occasionally exercises and tests his blood sugar in the morning. He reports that his blood sugars have been elevated lately. He is very active in his community and enjoys skiing.\n\nThe patient has a family history of diabetes.\n\nI have reviewed the patient\'s x-rays of his lumbar spine. These show no significant findings.\n\nI have reviewed the patient\'s vital signs. These are within normal limits.\n\nMy impression of the patient\'s symptoms is musculoskeletal back pain. I have recommended that we obtain an x-ray of his lumbar spine as an outpatient. I have also recommended that we obtain a CT scan of his lumbar spine if the x-ray is inconclusive. I have recommended that we refer him to a pain specialist if his symptoms do not improve.\n\nI have also reviewed his diabetes. I have recommended that he continue his current medications. I have recommended that he obtain an A1c today. I have also recommended that he monitor his blood sugars in the morning.\n\nThe patient is to take 400 mg of ibuprofen with Tylenol as needed for pain, up to 3 times a day. He is also to take Flexerel 5 mg once a day.\n\nThe patient is to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking Flexerel.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah Thompson is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began about a week ago. He is unsure if he injured himself, but he thinks he may have twisted his back to the left. The pain is located in the middle of his back, and radiates to the left side. The pain is aggravated with sudden movement to the left. He has tried over the counter pain medications with minimal improvement. His pain is 8/10.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes, which is currently under control. He is currently taking metformin twice daily. He does try to exercise and monitor his blood sugar, but he also enjoys skiing and states that he does not always check his blood sugar in the morning. His blood sugar levels have been elevated recently.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been doing well with his diabetes. He is currently taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEYES\n- Examination: Normal exam.\n\nEARS\n- Examination: Normal exam.\n\nRESPIRATORY\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCARDIOVASCULAR\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nNEURO\n- Muscle strength and tone: Normal gait.\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the back: Pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. Pain to left leg lift. Positive straight leg raise on the left.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine was ordered today.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nNoah Thompson is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. His onset began about a week ago. He is unsure if he injured himself, but he thinks he may have twisted his back to the left. The pain is located in the middle of his back, and radiates to the left side. The pain is aggravated with sudden movement to the left. He has tried over the counter pain medications with minimal improvement. His pain is 8/10.\n\nThe patient has a history of diabetes, which is currently under control. He is currently taking metformin twice daily. He does try to exercise and monitor his blood sugar, but he also enjoys skiing and states that he does not always check his blood sugar in the morning. His blood sugar levels have been elevated recently.\n\nFor his low back pain, I believe this is a musculoskeletal strain. I have ordered a low back x-ray, which will be performed today as an outpatient. We will also refer him to physical therapy for strengthening and pain management.\n\nFor his diabetes, I believe this is well controlled. We will order an A1c to check his levels. Continue to follow the diet and exercise. He should continue to take metformin twice daily. He should also take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed, with Tylenol. He should take Flexeril 10 mg once a day at night. He should avoid any strenuous activities, especially after taking the Flexeril.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medications, such as an overuse of muscle relaxers if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving.\n- Medical Treatment: Low back x-ray ordered. Referral to physical therapy provided. He will take 400 mg of ibuprofen up to 3 times per day. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the cl