rts that she was experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath. She states that she had seen me approximately 6 months ago and was doing well at that time. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports walking her dog daily.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she is able to afford her medications and is compliant with her medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria or hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly or jugular venous distention.\n- Carotid: No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nExtremities\n- Edema: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Radial artery pulses are palpable. The cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG: Normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes consistent with recent STEMI.\n\nECHO: Reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is status post interior STEMI. Her ejection fraction is reduced to 35% on echocardiogram.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her disease and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to continue her aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, toprol 50 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab for exercise and education. Repeat echocardiogram in 2 months.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: Given her history of coronary artery disease and recent STEMI, it is likely that her left ventricular dysfunction is secondary to her myocardial infarction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her condition and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to continue her lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute on chronic heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities and a reduced ejection fraction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her condition and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to take lasix 40 mg once a day and aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Repeat labs.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her condition and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to consider nutrition counseling to reduce her salt intake.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior stemi.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he walks his dog every day. He is looking forward to the upcoming holiday.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight changes.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.\nSkin: Denies rash.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base. This is not concerning given the patient\'s history.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower extremity edema. Pitting edema 1+ on the right radial artery. Cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Right radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nRESULTS\n\nElectrocardiogram shows normal sinus rhythm, good R wave progression, and evolutionary changes consistent with a recent STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram reveals a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. There is moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is a known coronary artery disease patient who presented with a recent STEMI and underwent a stent placement.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue his current diet and exercise regimen. We discussed the importance of watching his sodium intake and he is agreeable to this. We also discussed the importance of medication compliance.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. Continue Lipitor 80 mg daily and continue Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: This is concerning, but given his compliance with medication, I believe he will recover.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that his heart function is concerning, but he is already being closely monitored. He is compliant with his medications and is active, so we will continue to monitor him closely.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Moderate heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid, so we are going to start him on a diuretic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that his heart failure is manageable and that he will recover. I also discussed the importance of medication compliance.\n- Medical Treatment: Start Lasix 40 mg once a day and Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: This is well-controlled, but we will refer him to a nutrition specialist to discuss diet options.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that his blood pressure is well-controlled, but we want to discuss diet options.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue as directed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nWe will repeat an echocardiogram in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up status post inferior STEMI.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nRCA stent 2018\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\naspirin 81 mg daily\nBrilinta 90 mg twice daily\nLipitor 80 mg daily\nToprol 50 mg daily\nlisinopril 20 mg daily\nmetformin\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral leg swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nWithin normal limits\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: No carotid bruits. A 3/6 systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left base.\n- Examination: Right radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n  