exam though there is a three over six systolic ejection murmur heard at the that i\'m hearing at the left base that\'s not like a huge deal especially considering like what you\'ve been through already in terms of the hospital but it is something that i wan na know in your chart when i listen to you your lungs your lungs are clear over here with your extremities i do notice you know some lower lower edema swelling sorry some lower swelling edema lower limb edema and it it is pitting one plus on your right radial artery the cath site is clean dry and intact without hematoma and i also your right radial artery pulses are palpable so i did review the results of your ekg and they show a normal sinus rhythm and a good r wave progression and a a evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a stemi one of the reasons though that we had you come in is that we noticed on your echo there is a reduced ejection fraction of thirty five percent we are a little bit concerned about that so let\'s talk about my assessment and plan for you your primary and like first diagnosis is gon na be coronary artery disease right you already have a history of that but you know we\'re gon na do some things to make we\'re gon na continue to evaluate that over time i want you to continue your aspirin eighty one milligrams daily and your brilinta ninety milligrams twice daily i wan na continue you on a high dose statin called lipitor and you\'re gon na be taking that eighty milligrams a day and we\'re gon na continue you on your toprol and that\'s gon na be fifty milligrams daily i am gon na refer you to cardiac rehab so we can get you some education and give you some confidence to get back to exercising regularly i know it was kinda scary to have such an episode but i promise like most of the patients i have love cardiac rehab and i think you\'ll do pretty well for your second diagnosis we have newly you have newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation what i think is like what that means is that you\'re you\'re pumping like the way that your heart is pumping is a bit concerning but i think like given your history that it will improve over time they got you into the cath lab pretty quickly so i think that the muscle is just kinda like stunned like surprised and since you\'re compliant with your meds i think you will recover we are gon na continue you on your lisinopril twenty milligrams daily however considering that you are retaining fluid i think that your third diagnosis is like we are crossing into acute heart failure i\'m gon na prescribe something called a diuretic it\'s called lasix and i do want to we need to take that forty milligrams once a day i wan na add aldactone twelve . five milligrams daily and i need you to get labs finally we will repeat another echo in about two months last but not least there is the hypertension your blood pressure seems fine i think with the aldactone that you\'ll tolerate that pretty well but we do want to maybe get you into some nutrition counseling to consider like what other diet options might be available to you maybe suggestions of things that you have n\'t thought about like cutting out meat especially any kind of preserved meat like pepperoni but maybe finding you some alternatives how does that sound\n[patient] that sounds good yeah so i\'m yeah as long as you tell me i\'m gon na get better i believe you\n[doctor] yes yeah i i strongly believe this is our best course of action and that you will get better given enough time i i see that you are making efforts i\'m really glad that you are out there walking and that you\'re cognizant of your diet and willing to make changes we will just you know monitor it over time okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior STEMI.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia Smith is a pleasant 67-year-old female who presents for a follow-up after an interior STEMI. She reports that she was experiencing chest pain and shortness of breath. She states that she had seen me approximately 6 months ago and was doing well at that time. She denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports walking her dog daily.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports that she is able to afford her medications and is compliant with her medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\nGenitourinary: Denies dysuria or hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or swelling.\nNeurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: Neck is supple without thyromegaly or jugular venous distention.\n- Carotid: No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nExtremities\n- Edema: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Radial artery pulses are palpable. The cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG: Normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes consistent with recent STEMI.\n\nECHO: Reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is status post interior STEMI. Her ejection fraction is reduced to 35% on echocardiogram.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her disease and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to continue her aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, toprol 50 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab for exercise and education. Repeat echocardiogram in 2 months.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: Given her history of coronary artery disease and recent STEMI, it is likely that her left ventricular dysfunction is secondary to her myocardial infarction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her condition and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to continue her lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute on chronic heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities and a reduced ejection fraction.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her condition and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to take lasix 40 mg once a day and aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n- Medical Treatment: Repeat labs.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressure is stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of her condition and the importance of medication compliance. She was advised to consider nutrition counseling to reduce her salt intake.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up after interior stemi.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he walks his dog every day. He is looking forward to the upcoming holiday.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight changes.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness.\nSkin: Denies rash.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs are within normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention. No c