ove around without any difficulty and has been sleeping well, including an afternoon nap.\n\nPAST HISTORY\n\nMedical\nHypertension.\nStage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports that he is a big cheese fan and loves to watch football. He states that he goes to parties on the weekends and enjoys pizza and chicken wings. He reports that he loves to watch the Dallas Cowboys.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nBumex 2 mg daily.\nCozaar 100 mg daily.\nNorvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\nGeneral Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nCardiovascular\nGrade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, stable.\n\nRespiratory\nClear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nExtremities\n2+ pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nAcute on chronic congestive heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of shortness of breath and lower extremity edema are consistent with heart failure. His diet indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension likely led to his current symptoms.\n• Additional Testing: Renal artery ultrasound to rule out secondary causes of kidney disease.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to monitor weight daily and call if more than 3 lbs in 2 days. Referral to a nutritionist for dietary education.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s hypertension may have contributed to his heart failure symptoms.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nStage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s kidney disease is stable at this time.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue current medications.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nFollow-up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nKevin Martin is a pleasant 82-year-old male with a past medical history significant for hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease who presents for hospital follow-up after an acute on chronic congestive heart failure (CHF) exacerbation.\n\nThe patient states that he has been trying to watch his diet as instructed. He notes that his ankles have been swelling lately. He also states that he has been short of breath and even had to call 911. He notes that the paramedics placed him on the special mask to breathe when he arrived at the emergency room. The emergency room doctor stated that his blood pressure was elevated.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been taking his medications but admits that he sometimes misses doses. He also reports that he has been watching his diet. He states that he loves cheese, pizza, and chicken wings and sometimes misses those.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been feeling well since his hospitalization. He states that he was given a diuretic in the hospital which made him go to the bathroom a lot. He also reports that he was given a medication for his blood pressure and that he feels much better now.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been watching his diet and taking his medications since he has been home. He states that he has been checking his blood pressure with a blood pressure cuff at home and that it has been good.\n\nThe patient denies chest pain since he has been home.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight changes.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain. Reports shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports ankle swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/72 mmHg.\nHeart rate: 79 bpm.\nRespiratory rate: 26.\nOxygen saturation: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Trace of edema in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Congestive heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: This appears to have been caused by dietary indiscretion and uncontrolled hypertension.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to watch his diet and to avoid salty foods. I also encouraged him to cut back on his pizza and chicken wings.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg once daily.\n\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: He has been compliant with his medications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to watch his diet.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg once daily and continue Norvasc 5 mg once daily. Order renal artery ultrasound.\n\n3. Chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: We need to get more labs to ensure he is tolerating his regimen.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue current regimen.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHospital follow-up after acute on chronic CHF exacerbation.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports being a football fan.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking Bumex 2 mg once daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg once daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral ankle swelling.\nNeurological: Denies any sleep disturbance.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 128/72.\nHR: 79 bpm.\nRR: 26.\nSpO2: 99%.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distension noted.\n\