lizabeth Smith is a 53-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of chest pain. The patient reports that she has been having chest pain for the past 3 months. The pain is described as a dull pressure that occurs mainly when she walks. The patient denies that she is having chest pain at this moment. The patient reports that she is unable to exercise due to the chest pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain. Denies shortness of breath, sweating, or clamminess.\nEndocrine: Reports high blood sugar. Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No crackles or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No jugular venous distention. Peripheral pulses are equal.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft. No tenderness, guarding, or masses.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG was reviewed today and was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and diabetes. Her EKG today was normal. She has a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, but this has been present in the past. I suspect that she may have unstable angina. The patient has a family history of heart disease.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the diagnosis to the patient today. I explained that she may need to go to the hospital if her chest pain does not resolve with nitroglycerin. I advised her to call 911 if her chest pain does not resolve with the nitroglycerin.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to start her on aspirin 81 mg daily. I am also going to give her a prescription for nitroglycerin to take if she has chest pain. She should take one pill and wait 5 minutes to see if her chest pain resolves. If it does not 