p and the sweet things and once we have your heart taken care of or make sure your heart is okay you can and we can hopefully resolve this chest pain that you\'re having get back to your exercise regimen okay any questions about that\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and for the third diagnosis the high blood pressure you you do have a trend i\'ve noticed in your chart that your blood pressure\'s been running a little bit high several other people have documented as well i\'m gon na go ahead and start you on some hydrochlorothiazide it\'s a pretty low dose medication to help you with your blood pressure plus it\'ll also help with you some of the swelling you have in your in your feet i\'m gon na have you use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter your findings at epic mychart and i\'ll get a reading of those as well and then what when you come back in two weeks we\'ll keep a log of your blood pressure daily in the morning when you wake up and see how things are going and then if we need to make any adjustments to the medications we can but especially considering your history and the chest pain and the diabetes and the swelling i think starting this hydrochlorothiazide is a probably a good idea\n[patient] okay are there any side effects with this medication\n[doctor] yeah it can cause sometimes it can cause a little bit of make you go to the bathroom a little bit more often in the evening but generally most people do n\'t have a whole lot of side effects i\'ll give you a handout of the side effects but sometimes you can have a cross reaction with some other medications like sulfa medications but generally most people tolerate this pretty well\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright any other questions we got\n[patient] that\'s it\n[doctor] alright well great seeing you thanks for coming in today\n[patient] thank you appreciate it\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nElizabeth Smith is a 53-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of chest pain. The patient reports that she has been having chest pain for the past 3 months. The pain is described as a dull pressure that occurs mainly when she walks. The patient denies that she is having chest pain at this moment. The patient reports that she is unable to exercise due to the chest pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain. Denies shortness of breath, sweating, or clamminess.\nEndocrine: Reports high blood sugar. Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No crackles or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur. Regular rate and rhythm. No jugular venous distention. Peripheral pulses are equal.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft. No tenderness, guarding, or masses.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ pitting edema in the lower extremities. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG was reviewed today and was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and diabetes. Her EKG today was normal. She has a 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, but this has been present in the past. I suspect that she may have unstable angina. The patient has a family history of heart disease.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the diagnosis to the patient today. I explained that she may need to go to the hospital if her chest pain does not resolve with nitroglycerin. I advised her to call 911 if her chest pain does not resolve with the nitroglycerin.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to start her on aspirin 81 mg daily. I am also going to give her a prescription for nitroglycerin to take if she has chest pain. She should take one pill and wait 5 minutes to see if her chest pain resolves. If it does not resolve, she should take another pill and wait another 5 minutes and then another pill. If it still does not resolve, she should go to the hospital.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been doing fairly well with her diabetes. Her hemoglobin A1c was 7.0 last time. She is compliant with metformin 500 mg twice a day.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue to avoid pop and sweet things. I advised her to continue to see ophthalmology for diabetic retinopathy.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take metformin 500 mg twice a day. I will order another hemoglobin A1c today.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a trend of elevated blood pressure in her records. She has been seen by other clinicians who have documented elevated blood pressure.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I explained the diagnosis to the patient today. I advised her that she will need to monitor her blood pressure at home.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to start her on hydrochlorothiazide. I will have her use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter her findings in Epic MyChart. I will review her blood pressure log at her next visit.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nElizabeth Lee is a 53-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of chest pain. The onset of her chest pain began approximately 3 months ago. She states that she experiences chest pain when she is walking. The pain is described as a dull pressure. She denies chest pain at this time. She denies shortness of breath, diaphoresis, or any other associated symptoms. She denies any chest pain at this time. She states she is able to manage her diabetes with metformin 500 mg twice daily. She has an ophthalmology appointment in 2 weeks. She denies any nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or any side effects from her metformin. She denies any history of high blood pressure. She denies any family history of heart disease.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea.\nGenitourinary: Denies incontinence.\nNeurological: Denies headaches.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No crackles, rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. 2/6 systolic ejection murmur.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination: Soft abdomen. No tenderness, guarding, or mass. No hepatosplenomegaly.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ pitting edema of the bilateral lower extremities. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG from today is normal. No signs of a myocardial infarction.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient is a 53-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of chest pain. She has a history of diabetes and hypertension. She has a family history of heart disease. Her EKG today was normal and did not show any signs of a myocardial infarction. I have explained the possible diagnosis of unstable angina. I have referred her to cardiology for further evaluation. I have started her on a baby aspirin 81 mg daily and nitroglycerin as needed. She will take 1-3 tablets every 5 minutes until her symptoms resolve or she seeks medical attention.\n\n2. Diabetes.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient is a 53-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of chest pain. She has a history of diabetes and hypertension. She has a family history of heart disease. She is currently on metformin 500 mg twice daily. She has an ophthalmology appointment in 2 weeks. I have advised her to continue with her eye exam and to continue her metformin. I have ordered a hemoglobin A1c.\n\n3. Hypertension.\nMedical Reasoning: The patient is a 53-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of chest pain. She has a history of diabetes and hypertension. She has a family history of heart disease. She has been noted to have a trend of elevated blood pressures in her chart. She has been started on hydrochlorothiazide. She will use a blood pressure cuff at home and enter her findings in Epic MyChart. She will also bring a log of her blood pressure readings to her next appointment.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nChest pain on exertion.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a medical history of diabetes type 2. She has not been formally diagnosed with hypertension, but her blood pressures have been trending high.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of heart disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies diaphoresis or clamminess.\nCardiovascular: Reports chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies dyspnea.\nGastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD, swelling, or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort:\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 systolic ejection murmur, otherwise normal rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: No masses or tenderness. Soft, no guarding.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema in the ankles. No calf tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG was obtained and reviewed in office today and is unremarkable. Hemoglobin A1c: 7.0\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Chest pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: Considering her history of diabetes, possible history of hypertension, and family history of heart disease, I suspect her chest pain is likely unstable angina. Her recent EKG was unremarkable and there are no other signs of heart attack, but I am worried she may have some sort of a blockage causing her pain.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed proper protocol for sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. I advised her to go to the emergency department if the medication is not effective after approximately 5 minutes.\n- Medical Treatment: We will have her start a regimen of baby aspirin at 81 mg daily, for which I have sent in a prescription today. I also sent a prescription for nitroglycerin up to 3 tablets every 5 minutes as needed. Finally, I am going to have her follow up with cardiology for further evaluation.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: She appears to be doing well on her current regimen.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to keep her appointment with ophthalmology for evaluation of diabetic retinopathy.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to put in an order for a repeat hemoglobin A1c to check for any needed medication adjustments and have her follow up in 2 weeks. In the meantime, I want her to continue with metformin 500 mg twice daily and dietary modifications.\n\n3. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Several providers, including myself, have noted a trend of elevated blood pressures in the patient\'s chart.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to monitor her blood pressures at home over the next 2 weeks and report her readings to me via MyChart. This should help us determine if any adjustments need to be made to her new medications. We discussed the possible side effects of hydrochlorothiazide how this will hopefully reduce some of her swelling seen on physical exam. All of her questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: I\'m going to have her start hydrochlorothiazide at a low dose.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
